From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 22:46:34 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] uml: describe the arm64 virtual CPU in /proc/cpuinfo as arm64
 Linux does

An arm64 task read /proc/cpuinfo in x86 UML's layout: a "flags" line where
arm64 Linux prints "Features", ten hand-picked names out of the two dozen
bits AT_HWCAP and AT_HWCAP2 carry, and no CPU identification at all.
LLVM's sys::getHostCPUFeatures() reads the "Features" line on
Linux/AArch64 and found none, so it reported no host features, and
llvmpipe, -mcpu=native and every other client of that query generated
code for a lesser CPU than the one the guest runs on.

Print the file arch/arm64/kernel/cpuinfo.c prints for a 64-bit task.
"Features" names exactly the AT_HWCAP and AT_HWCAP2 bits every task
receives, in arm64 Linux's order and spelling: the names are arm64
Linux's own hwcap_str table, which the build extracts from cpuinfo.c, so
there is no copy to drift. The identification lines decode the MIDR_EL1
value the emulator returns to MRS; the kernel runs natively and cannot
read the emulated register, so it mirrors the value, and "CPU
architecture" is 8, as arm64 Linux always prints it. There is no model
name: arm64 Linux prints one only to a 32-bit task.

BogoMIPS is the value arm64 Linux derives from the architected timer,
DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST(CNTFRQ_EL0, HZ) as loops_per_jiffy, read from the
counter register a task reads. UML's busy-loop calibration still times
udelay(), but its result varied from boot to boot and between the
emulator's engines, and it no longer reaches the file.

The Darwin host no longer synthesizes a feature line for the kernel to
match by substring, and the hand-written table of ten names goes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
